168
00:23:20,609 --> 00:23:23,990
Henry do you want to talk through this?

169
00:23:25,660 --> 00:23:37,660
Yes this is quite outdated for old exposures right at the beginning of this whole process but we looked at a number of different regimes based on the exposure information we were given.

170
00:23:37,660 --> 00:23:50,730
And this is just an example of something that was in the original tender when we first put this together which is using different spectral acceleration regimes to model different exposures.

171
00:23:51,730 --> 00:23:55,730
So either using the 0.3 second or 1.0 second.

172
00:23:55,730 --> 00:23:58,730
So this is only considered at the time.

173
00:23:58,730 --> 00:24:07,730
I think what's important to note is that the payout table that is chosen is not specifically just the one that fits bets to the underlying exposures.

174
00:24:07,730 --> 00:24:19,730
But it also is chosen for reasons of balancing price, balancing that guaranteed 100% payouts at relatively no PGA values.

175
00:24:19,730 --> 00:24:32,819
So it's indexed much more for those larger scale events where anything north of 0.75% or 75% G will trigger 100% of the sell value.

176
00:24:32,819 --> 00:24:45,819
The table is a result not only of kind of exercise like this where we kind of modeled everything but we specifically chose that we did not want to overfit to any given disaster catastrophe model.

177
00:24:45,819 --> 00:24:54,180
Given that we didn't want to present too much faith in the underlying loss of course that's what's on.

178
00:24:54,180 --> 00:24:59,180
So there's lots of decisions that have gone into the payout table that has been chosen.

179
00:24:59,180 --> 00:25:02,180
I suppose it's been a course of months.

180
00:25:02,180 --> 00:25:14,269
We felt that the PGA table that has been chosen it cross-correlates very well with very well-known intensity regimes like Modified Macaulay Intensity Scale.

181
00:25:14,269 --> 00:25:27,269
It is designed to tap out at relatively low PGA's and it's also designed to balance costs by attaching at 0.35 which is perhaps slightly higher than when the first damages occur.

182
00:25:27,269 --> 00:25:34,269
We felt that it was likely that there would be significant cracks or damaging concrete buildings so that's essentially the rationale.

183
00:25:34,269 --> 00:25:41,269
We deliberately chose not to overfit some modelled curves like this but this was something that was presented to Terry and presented months and months ago.

184
00:25:41,269 --> 00:25:54,400
I don't want to speak for people on this call but the consensus that this was a bit too complicated a bit too much too soon and maybe this can be revisited in later years.

185
00:25:55,400 --> 00:26:00,400
That was many months ago now but is that what you had in mind at all?

186
00:26:00,400 --> 00:26:06,400
Thank you. There are several things after your explanations.

187
00:26:06,400 --> 00:26:15,400
Firstly, when you compare MMI with PGA and you see that there is a good correlation of MMI and PGA for small MMI values

188
00:26:15,400 --> 00:26:29,400
and then when you increase the value of MMI then PGA starts becoming a poorer correlation between MMI and PGA.

189
00:26:29,400 --> 00:26:46,400
I'm not really the fan of using MMI as the ground motion testimetric because it is very much sort of dependent of the observations

190
00:26:46,400 --> 00:26:55,400
so in that sense an observed ground motion metric is much better in structuring the payouts.

191
00:26:55,400 --> 00:27:06,400
The other thing is when we consider T-CIP it is only the structural damage.

192
00:27:06,400 --> 00:27:20,529
